\textbf{Keywords:} Layout Generation, CICPY, Placement, Routing,
Automation

\section{Layout}\label{layout}

The open source tools don't have any automatic analog layout. To my
knowledge, there is no general purpose analog automagic layout anywhere
in the world. It's an unsolved problem. Many have tried (including
myself), but none have succeeded with a generic analog layout engine.

There are a few things, though, that could help you on the way.

\section{Setup}\label{setup}

I assume that you have the latest and greatest
\texttt{aicex\textbackslash{}ip} setup.

See \href{https://analogicus.com/aic2026/sky130nm_tutorial}{SKY130NM
Tutorial} if aicex is unfamiliar.

Let's assume we use \texttt{jnw\_gr05\_sky130a} to test out our layout

\begin{Shaded}
\begin{Highlighting}[]
\BuiltInTok{cd}\NormalTok{ aicex/ip/}
\BuiltInTok{cd}\NormalTok{ jnw\_gr05\_sky130a}
\FunctionTok{git}\NormalTok{ checkout a1e3dfc324194729e042f5e653777b052759863b}
\BuiltInTok{cd}\NormalTok{ work}
\end{Highlighting}
\end{Shaded}

\section{CICPY}\label{cicpy}

The first thing we need to do is to place all transistors. I do have a
script to help. Install cicpy.

\begin{Shaded}
\begin{Highlighting}[]
\BuiltInTok{cd}\NormalTok{ aicex/ip/cicpy}
\FunctionTok{git}\NormalTok{ checkout master}
\FunctionTok{git}\NormalTok{ pull }
\ExtensionTok{python3} \AttributeTok{{-}m}\NormalTok{ pip install }\AttributeTok{{-}e}\NormalTok{ .}
\BuiltInTok{cd}\NormalTok{ ..}
\BuiltInTok{cd}\NormalTok{ cicspi }
\FunctionTok{git}\NormalTok{ checkout main }
\FunctionTok{git}\NormalTok{ pull}
\ExtensionTok{python3} \AttributeTok{{-}m}\NormalTok{ pip install }\AttributeTok{{-}e}\NormalTok{ .}
\end{Highlighting}
\end{Shaded}

\section{Placement}\label{placement}

To generate an initial placement we can do the command below. If a
layout exists it will be overridden

\begin{Shaded}
\begin{Highlighting}[]
\BuiltInTok{cd}\NormalTok{ jnw\_gr05\_sky130a/work }
\ExtensionTok{cicpy}\NormalTok{ sch2mag JNW\_GR05\_SKY130A OTA\_Manuel}
\end{Highlighting}
\end{Shaded}

\pandocbounded{\includegraphics[keepaspectratio]{media/layout_ota_m1.png}}

\emph{Figure 1: Initial cicpy sch2mag placement of OTA\_Manuel in Magic
- all transistors in one row, 19 DRC errors}

The layout engine has no idea what components belong together, for
example, the current mirror below should have been placed together

\pandocbounded{\includegraphics[keepaspectratio]{media/sch_ota_m1.png}}

\emph{Figure 2: Xschem schematic of the OTA bias circuitry, where the
current mirror pair xa07/xa20 should have been placed together}

We can instruct the layout engine by adding a ``group'' name to the
instance name. The instance name always starts with
\texttt{x\textless{}something\textgreater{}\textless{}number\textgreater{}}
where the something can be nothing, or a group name (a,b, not a number).

The rules for placement are:

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\tightlist
\item
  Sort all instances by groups
\item
  Sort all groups by instance name
\item
  Place the first instance.
\item
  For all instances: If the next instance has the same group, then add
  on top. Otherwise increment the x location.
\end{enumerate}

As such, if I rename my instances, as shown below,

\pandocbounded{\includegraphics[keepaspectratio]{media/sch_ota_m2.png}}

\emph{Figure 3: The same OTA schematic after renaming instances with
group prefixes (xa, xb, xd, xf, xg) to guide the placer}

Then the layout becomes a bit better

\begin{Shaded}
\begin{Highlighting}[]
\ExtensionTok{cicpy}\NormalTok{ sch2mag JNW\_GR05\_SKY130A OTA\_Manuel }\AttributeTok{{-}{-}gbreak}\NormalTok{ 3 }\AttributeTok{{-}{-}xspace}\NormalTok{ 34000 }\AttributeTok{{-}{-}yspace}\NormalTok{ 30000}
\end{Highlighting}
\end{Shaded}

The gbreak command inserts a ``group break'' after the fourth group,
such that a new Y coordinate is selected.

The X and Y space is for the distance between groups. The unit is
``Ångstrøm'', so 1 um is 10 000 Å.

\pandocbounded{\includegraphics[keepaspectratio]{media/layout_ota_m2.png}}

\emph{Figure 4: Placement after grouping, shown as instance boxes:
devices of the same group stack vertically, and --gbreak 3 starts a new
row}

\pandocbounded{\includegraphics[keepaspectratio]{media/layout_ota_m3.png}}

\emph{Figure 5: The same grouped placement with all layers drawn, DRC
clean}

\section{Summary}\label{summary}

The one-page version of this chapter:

\begin{itemize}
\tightlist
\item
  Layout is the final translation: from schematic to the polygons the
  fab will etch
\item
  Matching is geometry: symmetry, proximity, dummies, and common
  centroid where it counts
\item
  The parasitics are part of the circuit - extract and re-simulate
  before believing any layout
\item
  DRC and LVS are not suggestions: clean both, every time
\end{itemize}

